# Status (2026-10-12): fixed; set_body replaces the generated setter (test/samples/user_setter.sake).
# A user function named like an attr_accessor's generated setter is rejected with a message that calls the
# setter "a built-in operation". Ruby allows overriding `body=` (Sinatra::Response#body= overrides
# Rack::Response's); here the copy from `class B < A` brings A's setter, and defining set_body in B says:
#   error: `B.set_body` is a built-in operation and cannot be redefined
# Expected: either allow it (B's own definition wins, as for other copied functions) or say that set_body is
# the setter generated by `attr_accessor body` (copied from A).
class A
  attr_accessor body
end
class B < A
  def set_body(b, v)
    @body = (v in String) ? Array[v] : v
    v
  end
end
b = B.new(Array["x"])
B.set_body(b, "y")
p(B.body(b))
